---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Lightbox from "../../components/Lightbox.astro";
import SectionNav from "../../components/SectionNav.astro";
import { slugify } from "../../lib/slug.js";
import { createCitations } from "../../lib/references.js";
import { loadBib } from "../../lib/bib.js";
import Publication from "../../components/Publication-list.astro";
import "../../styles/more-link.css";
import "../../styles/citations.css";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";
import katex from "katex";
import topics from "../../data/research.json";

/* -------------------------
   SELECTED PUBLICATIONS

   Section 7 lists a few of my own papers. They come from
   papers.bib — the same file /publications reads — so the
   two can never disagree about a title or a page range.

   An unknown key throws rather than silently rendering an
   empty card.
------------------------- */
const papersByKey = new Map(
  loadBib("./src/data/papers.bib").map((p) => [p.key, p])
);

const selectPapers = (keys = []) =>
  keys.map((key) => {
    const paper = papersByKey.get(key);
    if (!paper) {
      throw new Error(
        `Unknown publication key "${key}". ` +
          `Check src/data/papers.bib. Known keys: ` +
          [...papersByKey.keys()].join(", ")
      );
    }
    return paper;
  });

/* -------------------------
   CITATIONS

   Three forms, mirroring natbib, because the difference is
   grammatical rather than decorative:

     [[hurst1951]]      → (Hurst, 1951)      \citep
                          an aside; the sentence stands without it

     [[t:granger1980]]  → Granger (1980)     \citet
                          the author is a noun in the sentence

     [[b:rue2009]]      → Rue et al., 2009   \citealp
                          no brackets of its own, for use inside
                          brackets already open: "(INLA, …)"

   Several keys in one marker share one bracket group:
     [[gistemp2018; lenssen2019]]
       → (GISTEMP Team, 2018; Lenssen et al., 2019)

   Two bibliographies, both listed under References:
   references.bib for other people's work, papers.bib for my
   own. Only what the page actually cites is listed, so the
   other entries in papers.bib stay out of it.

   An unknown key in either fails the build.

   Each page makes its own recorder, so the reference list
   below contains exactly the works this page cites — no
   orphans, and nothing missing.
------------------------- */
const { expandCitations, bibliography } = createCitations({
  sources: [
    { path: "./src/data/references.bib", anchor: "ref" },
    { path: "./src/data/papers.bib", anchor: "ref" }
  ]
});

/* -------------------------
   SAFE text renderer (NO math)
------------------------- */
const renderText = (text = "") => {
  return text.replace(/\$(.+?)\$/g, (_, expr) => {
    return katex.renderToString(expr, {
      throwOnError: false
    });
  });
};

/* -------------------------
   Math renderer (ONLY math)
------------------------- */
const renderMath = (expr = "") =>
  katex.renderToString(expr, {
    throwOnError: false,

    /* Without this KaTeX renders in text style, the same as
       an inline $x$: sums and integrals keep their limits
       tucked beside the operator instead of above and below
       it, and fractions are set at the smaller script size.
       Display style is what these blocks were always meant
       to be. It also wraps the output in .katex-display,
       which centres it as its own block. */
    displayMode: true
  });

/* -------------------------
   Select this topic
------------------------- */
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.id === "longmemory");

/* -------------------------
   PLACEHOLDER CONTENT (for learning)
------------------------- */
const demoContent = [
{
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "1. Background"
  },
    {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "After finishing my master's thesis at NTNU in September 2016, I started my PhD at UiT The Arctic University of Norway, under the supervision of Sigrunn Holbek Sørbye (UiT), and co-supervision of Håvard Rue (KAUST) and Martin Rypdal (UiT). The main aim of my PhD thesis was to develop an efficient approximation of long-memory processes that allows for fast Bayesian inference, and to use this methodology to analyze global mean surface temperature records. In addition to my supervisors, I also collaborated with Hege-Beate Fredriksen (UiT) and Kristoffer Rypdal (UiT)."
  },
  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "2. Introduction"
  },
  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 3,
    value: "2.1. Fractional Gaussian noise"
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "Long-range dependence (LRD) or long memory is a property relating to the rate of decay of statistical dependence of two observations as the distance between them increases. A common definition for long-memory behavior is given by a hyperbolic (as opposed to an exponential) decay of the autocovariance function."
  },

  {
    type: "math",
    value: "\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t, X_{t+k}) \\sim k^{2H - 2} ."
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "Here, $H$ measures the degree of long-range dependence and is commonly referred to as the Hurst exponent. The exponent is named after H.E. Hurst, who, while studying the storage requirements of reservoirs on the Nile River, observed that the rescaled range statistic of hydrological records followed a power-law [[hurst1951]], which could not be explained using contemporary short memory models. Hurst's results led to increased interest in the topic, and both hydrologists and mathematicians tried to find theoretical explanations of the so-called <em>Hurst phenomenon</em>. "
    
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "A major breakthrough came with the introduction of fractional Brownian motion and its increment process, fractional Gaussian noise (fGn), by [[t:mandelbrot1968]]. This is a process with an autocovariance function that decays hyperbolically "
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "\\gamma_H(k) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( (k+1)^{2H} - 2k^{2H} + (k-1)^{2H} \\right)."

  },
{
    type: "text",
    value:
      "The fractional Gaussian noise has since become a widely used model for long-memory behaviour, with applications in fields such as hydrology, dendrochronology, finance, among others. For my thesis I focused on applications in climate science, specifically on global mean surface temperature records, where long memory has been observed [[rypdal2016; rybski2006; lovejoy2013; huybers2006; franzke2010; fredriksen2016]]. "
  },
{
    type: "heading",
    level: 3,
    value: "2.2. Efficient Bayesian inference"
  },

  {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "It can be shown (see e.g. Theorem 2.2 in [[b:rue2005]]) that if two variables $x_i$ and $x_j$, in a Gaussian random field $\\bm x$, are conditionally independent given the other variables in the field, $\\bm{x}_{-ij}$, the corresponding term in the precision matrix is zero, i.e. $Q_{ij}=0$. Thus, if $\\bm x$ has a high degree of conditional independence we say that it is a Gaussian Markov random field (GMRF) and the precision matrix (inverse covariance matrix) will then be sparse. For example, since each node of an AR(1) process is only conditionally dependent on its immediate predecessor it is a GMRF with a tridiagonal precision matrix." 
  },

  {
    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/ar1graph.svg",
    caption:
      "Conditional dependence structure for an AR(1) process. Each node only depends (directly) on its immediate predecessor. This Markov property ensures a sparse precision matrix which allows for the use of computationally efficient algorithms."
  },
{
    type: "text",
    value: "The sparse precision matrix associated with the Markov property allows for many efficient algorithms for key operations to be utilized. This includes computing the log-likelihood [[rue2001]], performing Cholesky factorization and producing samples. This is particularly important for Bayesian inference, where the posterior distributions are typically evaluated using time-consuming sampling-based methods such as Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC). The Markov property allows for these methods to be implemented more efficiently, but also allows for more efficient alternatives such as integrated nested Laplace approximations (INLA, [[b:rue2009]]). INLA utilizes the sparse structure of the precision matrix to compute the posterior marginal distributions numerically, foregoing the need for sampling. "
},
   {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "Unlike Markov processes like the AR(1), long-memory processes exhibit persistent correlations over extended time periods, where each variable is conditionally dependent on all other variables. This means that the precision matrix is dense, and key inference algorithms have a computational cost that scales cubically with the number of observations, making them impractical for large datasets. In simple cases it is possible to exploit the Toeplitz structure of the covariance matrix to achieve $\\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ cost, but in many situations, such as when we have inhomogeneous variance, the Toeplitz structure breaks and the cost increases to $\\mathcal{O}(n^3)$. This motivates the need for an approximate model that can capture the long range dependence of the fGn process while retaining the Markov property, allowing for efficient inference algorithms to be utilized."
  },
  {
    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/fgngraph.svg",
    caption:
      "Conditional dependence structure for an fGn process. All nodes have a direct dependence with all other nodes. This makes inference, and many important matrix operations, computationally expensive."
  },

  {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "[[t:granger1980]] showed that if the lag-one autocorrelation parameter $\\phi$ of an AR(1) process is sampled from a beta distribution the aggregation of AR(1) processes would exhibit long-range dependence according to a fractionally integrated process. It should therefore, in theory, be possible to express an fGn process through a mixture of short memory processes, which would grant the Markov property and possibly reduce computational cost. "
  },

{
    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/fgnapproxgraph.svg",
    caption:
      "Conditional dependence structure for an approximate fGn process, expressed as a mixture of four AR(1) processes. The dependency between different nodes of the fGn process (orange) is expressed only through the individual AR(1) processes (blue). This makes the approximate model inherit the Markov property, allowing for efficient inference."
  },
{
    type: "text",
    value:
      "Unfortunately, as pointed out by [[t:haldrup2017]], the number of AR(1) processes (with $\\phi$ drawn from a beta distribution) required to exhibit long-range dependence is so large that any gains in the Markov property would be lost to the increase in variables associated with the high number of AR(1) processes, making a sampling-based approach impractical for inference. "
  },

{
    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/sparsity.png",
    caption:
      "Sparsity pattern of the precision matrix of (a) an AR(1) process, (b) an fGn process and (c) an approximate fGn as a mixture of 4 AR(1) processes. In (d), the AR(1) mixture process has been reordered such that the precision matrix becomes a band matrix."
  },
















  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "3. AR(1) mixture approximation"
  },

  
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "In [[t:Sorbye2019]] we propose a numerical approach to approximating long-range dependence by aggregating AR(1) processes. "+
      "Specifically, we approximate the fGn process $\\bm z$ by: "
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "\\tilde{\\boldsymbol{z}} = \\sum_{i=1}^{m} \\sqrt{w_i}\\boldsymbol{x}_i + \\tau"
    },
 {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "where $\\bm x_i$ denote independent AR(1) processes with unit variance and lag-one correlation $\\phi_i$. "+
      "To avoid a singular precision matrix for the joint GMRF $(\\tilde{\\bm{z}},\\bm x_1,...,\\bm x_m)$ we add a small noise term $\\tau\\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,\\exp(-15))$. "+
      "Instead of drawing the lag-one parameters from a random distribution we instead used numerical optimization to find the weights $\\bm w$ and lag-one parameters $\\bm \\phi$ which results in the autocorrelation function (acf), $\\gamma_{\\tilde{\\bm z}}$, which (up to a selected lag $k_{\\text{max}}$) best approximates the target acf, $\\gamma_H$, of an fGn with some Hurst exponent $H$,"
  },
{
    type: "math",
    value: "\\gamma_{\\tilde{\\bm z}}(k) = \\sum_{i=1}^m w_i \\phi_i^k."
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "Formally, the optimization problem can be expressed as: "
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "(\\boldsymbol{w},\\boldsymbol{\\phi})_H = \\underset{\\boldsymbol{w},\\boldsymbol{\\phi}}{\\arg\\min} \\; \\sum_{k=1}^{k_\\text{max}} \\frac{1}{k} \\left(\\gamma_{\\tilde{\\boldsymbol{z}}}(k)-\\gamma_{H}(k) \\right)^2"
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
      "and is repeated for all $H$ in a grid covering the persistent range, $H \\in (0.5, 1)$. The weighting term $1/k$ is used to give more importance to small lags in the optimization procedure, which are more important for estimation accuracy. The optimization yields a mapping from $H$ to a corresponding set of weights $\\bm w$ and lag-one autocorrelation parameters $\\bm \\phi$, which in turn gives us an approximate fGn process expressed as a mixture of $m$ AR(1) processes. We find that only using $m=4$ AR(1) processes in the aggregation provides excellent approximation of the autocorrelation function. We also find good estimation accuracy even for time series exceeding $k_{\\text{max}}=1000$. See [[t:Sorbye2019]] or [[t:myrvoll2020efficient]] for more details."
  },


  {
    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/fgnmap.svg",
    caption:
      "Panel (a) shows the autocorrelation function of an approximate fGn process, using 4 AR(1) processes, against the correlation function of a target fGn process with H = 0.8. The mapping from each H in the persistent range of (0.5, 1) to the corresponding weights and lag-one autocorrelation parameters are shown in panels (b) and (c), respectively."
  },
{
    type: "text",
    value:
    "To fully exploit the computational advantages of a GMRF approximation of an fGn we formulate a latent Gaussian model, and perform full Bayesian inference using INLA through its implementation in R (<a href='https://www.r-inla.org' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer'>r-inla.org</a>). Being able to incorporate the model into the R-INLA framework allows us to easily add other model components such as seasonal trends and random effects. The model has since been incorporated into R-INLA as a standard model.  "
  },
{
    type: "text",
    value:
    "The approximate fGn model laid the foundation for my PhD thesis, and was subsequently applied to a number of real-world problems in climate science. "
  },

















  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "4. Incorporating radiative forcing"
  },



{
    type: "text",
    value:
    "Reliable quantification of the global mean surface temperature (GMST) response to radiative forcing is essential for assessing the risk of dangerous anthropogenic climate change. For time scales ranging from months to centuries this response can be expressed as scale-invariant [[rypdal2016; rybski2006; lovejoy2013; huybers2006; franzke2010; fredriksen2016]]" +
    ", implying long memory properties."
  },
{
    type: "text",
    value:
    "To understand how radiative forcing affects the GMST we can use a simple energy balance model, which describes the change in heat content of the climate system as a function of the radiative forcing $F$ and the heat loss to space. This can be expressed as "
  },
{
    type: "math",
    value: "\\frac{d\\Delta Q}{dt} = -\\lambda \\Delta T + F"
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "and $\\Delta Q = C\\Delta T$, where $\\Delta Q$ is the change in the system's heat content, $C$ is the heat capacity, $\\Delta T$ is the temperature anomaly and $\\lambda$ is the feedback parameter. The forcing can be decomposed into a known component $F_{\\text{known}}$, and an unknown, stochastic component $dB(t)$. By solving the energy balance model we find that the GMST can be expressed as the sum of two components. First, the response to known forcing "
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "\\mu (t) = \\frac{1}{C} \\int_0^t g(t-s) F_{\\text{known}}(s) ds"
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "where $g(t-s) = e^{-(t-s)\\lambda/C}$ is the Green's function. And second, an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process $\\bm \\varepsilon$ which describes the response to the stochastic forcing component "
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "\\varepsilon(t) = \\frac{\\sigma}{C} \\int_0^t g(t-s) dB(s) ."
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "When discretized, the stochastic component results in an AR(1) process. This model is, however, not consistent with observations [[rypdal2014]] due to the slow climate response associated with the energy exchange with the deep ocean. This simple energy balance model can be extended to an $m$-box model where heat is exchanged between $m$ 'boxes', representing different layers of the deep ocean. Mathematically, the $m$-box model can be expressed as"
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "C\\frac{d\\Delta T(t)}{dt} = \\bm K \\Delta T(t) + F(t) ."
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "Here, $\\bm K$ is a matrix representing the heat exchange between the boxes. As the number of boxes increases towards infinity, the Green's function can be expressed as a scale invariant response function [[fredriksen2017]]"
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "g(t) = t^{H-3/2},"
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "which means that the response to the stochastic component can be expressed as a fractional Gaussian noise process with Hurst exponent $H$. Moreover, letting $F_0$ denote an unknown shift parameter in the known forcing, and $\\sigma$ and $\\sigma_f$ be scaling parameters for the stochastic and known forcing, respectively, we can express the GMST as "
  },
  {
    type: "math",
    value: "\\Delta \\bm T = \\bm \\mu(H,F_0,\\sigma_f) + \\bm \\varepsilon(H,\\sigma) ."
  },
  {
    type: "text",
    value:
    "Since none of the default R-INLA models support this specific formulation we need to construct the latent model component using the custom modeling framework of R-INLA called <em>rgeneric</em>. To make the temperature response model easily accessible to other researchers we have developed a user-friendly R-package called <em>INLA.climate</em>, which takes care of the technical implementation of the model and allows users to easily fit the model to their data using R-INLA. This package is available at my GitHub repository: <a href='https://github.com/eirikmn/INLA.climate' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer'>INLA.climate</a>, and a demonstration and short description is also available under the 'Software' section of this website. A more detailed description and tutorial of this package can be found in Appendix A in my PhD thesis [[myrvoll2020efficient]]. "
  },

  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "5. Climate applications"
  },

{
    type: "text",
    value:
    "There are several benefits of having the model specified through the R-INLA framework. For example, any missing values in the GMST will be imputed automatically using the posterior predictive distribution (but the known forcing has to be provided as input for all points)."+
    " This also means that we can very easily use the model for prediction. In [[t:myrvoll2020statistical]] we predict the GMST under the four Representation Concentration Pathways (RCP) provided by the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP) for the period 2006-2100, and find that the model provides good predictive performance even for long-term predictions up to 100 years into the future."
  },
  {
    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/rcp.svg",
    caption:
      "Panel (a) shows the Hansen Greenhouse Gas forcing extended with the four RCP scenarios. Panel (b) shows the HadCRUT global mean surface temperature with predicted posterior marginal mean and 95% credible intervals for each RCP scenario."
  },
{
    type: "text",
    value:
    "In other applications we use the model to estimate both the transient climate sensitivity [[myrvoll2020statistical]] and the equilibrium climate sensitivity [[rypdal2018emergent]], which are key metrics for understanding the risk of dangerous anthropogenic climate change. "+
    "In [[t:myrvoll2019warming]] we exploit the reduced computational cost in order to fit the model to all local temperature records in the spatio-temporal dataset, GISS Surface Temperature Analysis version 4 (GISTEMP v4) [[gistemp2018; lenssen2019]]."
  },
{

    type: "figure",
    src: "/images/research/longmemory/Hmap.png",
    caption:
      "Image taken from [[t:myrvoll2019warming]]. The posterior marginal mean estimate of the Hurst exponent obtained from fitting an fGn model using global radiative forcing to local time series data from the GISTEMP v4 spatio-temporal temperature dataset, for the years 1900–2015."
  },
  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "6. Relevant publications in this field"
  },
  {
    /* Pulled from papers.bib by citation key, and rendered
       with the same card the /publications page uses — so
       the abstracts, DOIs and PDFs come along, and nothing
       here can fall out of step with that page.

       Order is chronological and deliberate; it is whatever
       this array says, not whatever the .bib happens to
       contain. */
    type: "publications",
    keys: [
      "rypdal2018emergent",
      "Sorbye2019",
      "myrvoll2019warming",
      "myrvoll2020statistical",
      "myrvoll2020efficient"
    ]
  },
  {
    /* The five above are a selection, not the whole record —
       this is the route to the rest. Same treatment as the
       homepage's "All publications", so the two read as one
       site rather than two. */
    type: "html",
    value:
      `<p class="pub-selection-more"><a class="more-link" href="/publications"><span>All publications</span></a></p>`
  },

  {
    type: "heading",
    level: 2,
    value: "References"
  },
];




/* -------------------------
   EXPAND CITATIONS, THEN BUILD THE LIST

   Order matters: every [[key]] has to be expanded before
   bibliography() is called, because that is what records
   which works the page used. Doing it here rather than in
   the template also means an unknown key fails the build
   instead of half-rendering a page.
------------------------- */
const stripTags = (html = "") => String(html).replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "");

const content = demoContent.map((block) => {
  if (block.type === "text" || block.type === "html") {
    return { ...block, value: expandCitations(block.value) };
  }

  /* Captions can cite too — "Image taken from …". The caption
     becomes HTML once a citation is expanded, so it needs
     set:html in the template; captionText keeps a plain copy
     for the img alt, which must not contain markup. */
  if (block.type === "figure" && block.caption) {
    const caption = expandCitations(block.caption);
    return { ...block, caption, captionText: stripTags(caption) };
  }

  return block;
});

/* "html", not "text": the list is a <ul>, which cannot live
   inside a <p> — see the render branch below. */
content.push({ type: "html", value: bibliography() });

/* contents list, derived from the headings below so the two
   can never fall out of step */
const sections = content
  .filter((b) => b.type === "heading" && (b.level ?? 3) === 2)
  .map((b) => ({ label: b.value, id: slugify(b.value) }));
---

<Layout
  title={topic?.title ?? "Long-range dependence"}
  description="Efficient Bayesian inference for long-memory processes: approximating fractional Gaussian noise as a Gaussian Markov random field so that INLA can be used, with applications to global mean surface temperature."
>

  <div class="research-page">

    <h1>{topic?.title ?? "Efficient Bayesian analysis of long-memory processes with applications in climate."}</h1>

    {topic?.summary && (
      <p class="intro">{topic.summary}</p>
    )}

    <div class="research-layout">

      <div class="research-grid">

      <section class="research-card">

        <div class="content">
          {content.map((block) => {

            if (block.type === "text") {
              return (
                <p class="text" set:html={renderText(block.value)} />
              );
            }

            /* Block-level HTML — lists, paragraphs — has to go
               in a <div>, not a <p>. A <p> is auto-closed the
               moment the parser meets a <p> or <ul> inside it,
               which silently moves the content out of .text and
               breaks every descendant style rule aimed at it.
               Keeps class="text" so the prose measure, line
               height and colour still apply. */
            if (block.type === "html") {
              return (
                <div class="text" set:html={renderText(block.value)} />
              );
            }

            if (block.type === "heading") {
              const Tag = `h${block.level || 3}`;

              return (
                <Tag class="subheading" id={slugify(block.value)}>
                  {block.value}
                </Tag>
              );
            }

            if (block.type === "math") {
              return (
                <div
                  class="equation"
                  set:html={renderMath(block.value)}
                />
              );
            }

            if (block.type === "publications") {
              return (
                <div class="pub-selection">
                  {selectPapers(block.keys).map((paper) => (
                    <Publication
                      title={paper.title}
                      type={paper.type}
                      authors={paper.authors}
                      venue={paper.journal || paper.school}
                      year={paper.year}
                      abstract={paper.abstract}
                      doi={paper.doi}
                      pdf={paper.pdf}
                      url={paper.url}
                      volume={paper.volume}
                      number={paper.number}
                      pages={paper.pages}
                    />
                  ))}
                </div>
              );
            }

            if (block.type === "figure") {
              return (
                <figure class="figure">
                  <img
                    src={block.src}
                    alt={block.captionText ?? block.caption ?? ""}
                  />
                  {block.caption && (
                    <figcaption set:html={block.caption} />
                  )}
                </figure>
              );
            }

            return null;
          })}
        </div>

      </section>

      </div>

      <aside class="section-nav-col">
        <SectionNav items={sections} />
      </aside>

    </div>

  </div>

  <Lightbox />

</Layout>

<style>
/* Width, gutters, body typography and heading styles all
   come from .container and the global rules in
   Layout.astro — the same as every other page.
   Only this page's own components are styled here. */

.intro {
  color: var(--text-muted);
  max-width: 70ch;
  line-height: 1.5;
  margin-bottom: 2rem;
}

/* spacing */
.research-grid {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

/* .research-card intentionally unstyled: the accent spine
   and its 1rem indent were eating horizontal space on a
   full-width prose page. Spacing comes from .research-grid. */

/* text blocks */
.text {
  margin: 0.5rem 0;
  line-height: 1.6;
}

/* headings inside content */
.subheading {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}

/* equations */
/* `overflow-x: auto` made every equation a scroll container.
   KaTeX overflows its box by a pixel or two even when the
   formula visibly fits, which is enough for the wheel to be
   captured instead of scrolling the page. Equations fit the
   column on desktop, so the guard is only kept for narrow
   viewports where a wide formula really could overflow. */
.equation {
  margin: 1.4rem 0;
  overflow-x: visible;
}

/* KaTeX's own .katex-display carries margin: 1em 0. Left
   alone it stacks with .equation's margin and the spacing
   around every formula doubles. Let the wrapper own it. */
.equation :global(.katex-display) {
  margin: 0;
}

@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .equation {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
}

/* ✅ FIX FOR YOUR FIGURE SIZE */
/* Browsers default <figure> to `margin: 1em 40px`, costing
   80px of width. Invisible on desktop, where the image is
   capped at 600px well inside the column, but severe on a
   phone. Keep the vertical rhythm, drop the side margins. */
.figure {
  margin: 1em 0;
}

.figure img {
  max-width: 600px;   /* prevents huge images */
  width: 100%;        /* responsive */
  height: auto;
  display: block;
  margin: 1rem auto;  /* centers image */
  border-radius: 8px;
  border: 1px solid var(--border-ui);

  /* These plots are exported with transparent backgrounds and
     black axes, so on the dark theme they were drawing
     black-on-black. Every figure gets a plate.

     True white, now that the page itself is off-white. The
     plate used to be the off-white one at #fafafa against a
     #ffffff page, which made it marginally DARKER than its
     surroundings and left it reading as nothing. The two have
     swapped: the page is the gentle surface and the plate is
     the white the plots were exported on, so a figure lifts
     off the page rather than dissolving into it. */
  background: #ffffff;
  padding: 0.5rem;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

/* In dark mode a full-brightness plate glares against the
   #111 page. brightness() scales channel values, so black
   line art stays black while the light areas come down —
   contrast is preserved, glare is not. Applying it to the
   element dims the CSS plate and the SVG's own pale fills
   together, so they cannot drift apart. */
html.dark .figure img {
  filter: brightness(0.9);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------
   FIGURE NUMBERING

   A CSS counter, so numbers stay correct when figures are
   added, removed or reordered — nothing to maintain by hand.
   The counter is reset on the article wrapper so each page
   starts at Figure 1.
--------------------------------------------------------- */

.content {
  counter-reset: figure;
}

.figure {
  counter-increment: figure;
}

.figure figcaption {
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  margin-top: 0.4rem;
  line-height: 1.45;
}

.figure figcaption::before {
  content: "Figure " counter(figure) ". ";
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--text);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------
   SELECTED PUBLICATIONS

   Left uncapped rather than held to the 68ch prose measure.
   The cards carry a right-hand column of type badge and
   action buttons; squeezed to 68ch that column collides with
   the title. Like the figures, they read as deliberately
   wider than the running text.
--------------------------------------------------------- */

.pub-selection {
  margin: 1rem 0 0.5rem;
}

/* The last card's rule would otherwise sit immediately above
   the "full list" link and read as a divider between the
   link and the section that follows. */
.pub-selection :global(.pub:last-child) {
  border-bottom: none;
  margin-bottom: 0;
  padding-bottom: 0;
}

.text :global(.pub-selection-more) {
  margin: 1rem 0 0;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------
   READING MEASURE

   The shared column is 850px, which is about 106 characters
   per line at the body size — well past the 60-75 that is
   comfortable to read. Cap the running text, but leave
   figures and equations free to use the full width, so they
   read as deliberately wide rather than the prose looking
   stretched.
--------------------------------------------------------- */

.text,
.subheading,
.research-card > h2,
.research-card > h3 {
  max-width: 68ch;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------
   ARTICLE + TABLE OF CONTENTS

   The prose is capped at 68ch, so the right-hand slack in
   the 850px column was going unused. The contents list sits
   there and sticks while you read.
--------------------------------------------------------- */

.research-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 190px;
  gap: 2rem;
  align-items: start;
}

.section-nav-col {
  position: sticky;
  top: 96px;
  min-width: 0;
}

/* clear the sticky site header when jumping to a section */
.subheading {
  scroll-margin-top: 96px;
}

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .research-layout {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0;
  }

  /* contents above the article on narrow screens */
  .section-nav-col {
    position: static;
    order: -1;
  }
}

</style>